---
.title = "Transports",
.description = "The netlayers that move a session's bytes between two peers — TCP, keyed TCP, in-process, iroh, and WebRTC — and how the core stays ignorant of which one carried a frame.",
.date = @date("2026-06-12T00:00:00"),
.author = "Jim Isaacs",
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

A transport, or *netlayer*, moves session bytes between two peers. The session
above it does not change. In the table, the **designator** is the peer name
inside an OCapN locator.

| Transport | Symbol | Channel | Designator | Reach for it when |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| TCP | `tcp` | cleartext TCP | `host:port` | a real network, or Goblins interoperability |
| Keyed TCP | `keyed-tcp` | cleartext TCP, key-derived designator | Ed25519 pubkey hex; socket address in `direct-addresses` | restart-stable designators and key-squatter refusal ([ADR-0075](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0075-keyed-tcp-designator.md)) |
| In-process | `inproc` | in-memory byte pipes, no sockets | a name within one process | tests and demos that run both peers in one process |
| iroh | `iroh` | encrypted QUIC (separate artifact) | a self-authenticating node id in an `ocapn://` URI | you want an encrypted channel today, or peer-to-peer reach |
| WebRTC | `webrtc` | encrypted DataChannel over DTLS (separate artifact) | an Ed25519 designator reached through signaling | direct browser-to-browser traffic |

TCP and keyed TCP are not encrypted. Use iroh for encrypted native
peer-to-peer traffic and WebRTC for a direct browser channel. A browser can
also reach a native peer through the byte-transparent
[relay]($link.page('relay')).

[Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing')) turns the table into
a decision. The [iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')) and
[WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc')) pages cover setup and limits.
[Modules]($link.page('modules')) explains how netlayers plug into the driver.
